================================================================================
TUNNEL API FIX: native binary file-write action (`fsWriteBinary` / `writeBinary`)
================================================================================
Target repo : geelooy/apps/tunnel/agent   (Mac agent, split-agent-2.0.0)
Patch files : 3  (all under <repo>/geelooy/apps/tunnel/agent/)
Status      : WRITTEN ONLY — NOT DEPLOYED. User review required before deploy.
Date        : 2026-10-06

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PROBLEM
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
To upload a file through the tunnel today, clients must smuggle bytes through
the `commandRun` action as `echo $B64 | base64 -d > /path`. This is:

  1. Slow      — +33% base64 overhead AND 3 HTTPS round trips per chunk
                 (commandRun submit -> commandWait poll loop -> commandJobOutputPage).
  2. Size-capped — ~50 KB per call: the base64 blob is embedded in the shell
                 command string, so ARG_MAX / command-line limits apply.
  3. Wasteful   — a file write is forced through the durable async job system
                 (job store, output paging, ledger) built for long commands.

The agent ALREADY has atomic binary-safe write machinery
(`tools/fs/atomic-file-write.js: replaceFile()` accepts a Buffer, does
temp-file + fsync + atomic rename), but no public action exposes it for raw
bytes. The existing `write` action only takes text (`String(content)`).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FIX OVERVIEW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Add a native `fsWriteBinary` action (plus `writeBinary` alias) that:

  * accepts  { action:"fsWriteBinary", path:"/tmp/f", contentBase64:"..." }
  * decodes base64 -> Buffer IN-PROCESS (no shell, no `base64 -d` subprocess)
  * writes via the existing atomic `replaceFile()` path (temp + fsync + rename)
  * reuses the exact same guards as text writes:
      config.tools.fsWrite, config.allowWrite, safePath(), assertNotSecret()
  * returns immediately { ok:true, bytesWritten:N } — no job, no polling.
    (Action dispatch is a direct map lookup in actionRuntime.js; only the
    `command*` family uses the async job system. Plain fs actions already
    execute synchronously in the request.)

Naming / public-family mapping (important):
  * `writeBinary` matches the EXISTING family rule  /^write/i  -> "files",
    so it is reachable via the public API with ZERO server-side change.
  * `fsWriteBinary` (the name requested) needs one new agent-side rule
    /^fsWrite/i -> "files" (included below). The server (awtsmoos.com)
    mirrors family mapping (POLICY_VERSION 2); until its mirrored rule is
    updated, `fsWriteBinary` may classify as "system" server-side. The
    `writeBinary` alias works regardless — ship both, use `writeBinary`
    first, cut over to `fsWriteBinary` after the server rule lands.

Payload ceiling: agent local-api-limits.js sets BODY_LIMIT = 16 MB for JSON
bodies, so one call can carry ~12 MB of raw bytes after base64 inflation.
The 12 MB Meluket tarball fits in ONE request (vs 231 chunked commandRun
calls today). The tunnel control plane may impose a smaller limit — the
test plan below finds the real ceiling empirically.

BONUS (no agent deploy needed): `commandRun` ALREADY has a synchronous
path.  tools/fs/actionGroups/commandActionSync.js: `shouldRunSync(payload)`
returns true when payload has sync/inline/blocking truthy, and `runSmart`
then runs the command inline and returns stdout in the SAME response —
one round trip, no commandWait loop, no output-page fetch. Clients can use
{"action":"commandRun","sync":true,...} TODAY, provided the server passes
the `sync` field through (verify in testing).

